nal and institutional  development of this principle  and was the architect of the secretary  general's  three pillar  strategy for implementing the  responsibility to protect  as well as the the prime author of the  first four reports on on r2p  between 2009 and 2012 and clearly the  responsibility protect was one of the  the the concepts and the developments  that i think david and i had in mind  when we thought about a  continuing legacy  of  the events in rwanda  dr luck has published many books  particularly on the un's security  council on compliance with international  law  on u.s exceptionalism on arms control  and on the ending of of war i will  mention just a couple of the books one  is called on the endings of war another  is called arms control the multilateral  alternative  mixed messages american politics and  international organizations  he has taught at columbia at princeton  at cionus poe  he's headed research centers at columbia  and at the woodrow wilson school in  nyu school of law  he served as president and ceo of the un  association of the united states  and is a senior vice president of the  international peace  institute  he is also a  graduate  of dartmouth college where ba in  international relations and has his m.a  and phd in political science from  columbia and a master's in international  affairs i guess that's what that stands  for from colombia's school of  international and public affairs  to  his left from the direction you're  facing  is james traub  he writes a weekly column terms of  engagement for foreignpolicy.com  he's been a staff writer for the new  york times magazine and  previously for the new yorker  he's written extensively about  international affairs  but also about national politics  urban affairs with a number of books in  particular about urban affairs and about  new york and about education  he's written as they said in the new  york times magazine the new yorker but  in many other publications as well  recently 